<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Hanover</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1692">1692</a>–<a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> was a German princely dynasty, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> and later kings of Hanover, that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and Ireland from 1714 to 1901. The English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">Act of Settlement</a> of 1701 fixed the succession on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophia-of-hanover">Sophia of Hanover</a>, a Protestant granddaughter of James I, and her son became George I in 1714. The personal union with Hanover ended at Victoria's accession in 1837.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover.jpg" alt="House of Hanover" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan; derivative work by Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Hanover_Inescutcheon.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> began in 1635 as a cadet branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a>, also called the House of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>. When the Duchy of Brunswick-Lüneburg was divided that year, Duke George inherited the Principality of Calenberg and moved his residence to Hanover. His sons shared Calenberg and Lüneburg among them until his grandson George united the two in 1705. The fourth son, Ernest Augustus, was raised to the rank of prince-elector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in 1692. His wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophia-of-hanover">Sophia of the Palatinate</a>, was named heiress to the English throne by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">Act of Settlement</a> of 1701, which barred Roman Catholics; she was then the senior eligible Protestant descendant of James I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sophia died in 1714, and her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-i-of-great-britain">George Louis</a> became George I of Great Britain that year. The dynasty gave six British monarchs: George I, George II, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a>, George IV, William IV and Victoria. George I, George II and George III were also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a>, and as rulers of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> they kept control of the Hanoverian army and foreign policy. Hanover became a kingdom in 1814, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>.</p><p>When William IV died in 1837 the personal union ended. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>'s succession followed a semi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Salic law</a> that gave priority to all male lines, so the throne passed not to Victoria but to her uncle Ernest Augustus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-king-of-hanover">Duke of Cumberland</a>, who reigned until 1851. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> was deposed in 1866 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed the kingdom, and he went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Victoria died in 1901 and the British crown passed to her son Edward VII of the House of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>.</p><p>The senior Welf line, which ruled Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>, died out in 1884. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> opposed a Hanoverian succession in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> until Ernest Augustus, the grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a>, married Princess Viktoria Luise of Prussia in 1913 and became duke that year; the marriage settled the quarrel between the houses. He was overthrown in 1918, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> became a republic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> gave the Prince-Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a> alternately to a Catholic bishop and to a cadet of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, so it became a kind of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of the house until it was mediatised to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> in 1803. Many places in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> were named for the house and its members, among them the American state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, New Brunswick in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, Victoria in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a>, and numerous towns called Hanover or Brunswick. Georgian architecture takes its name from the styles current between 1714 and 1830. The house is now the only surviving branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Hanover” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the English Act of Settlement of 1701 settled the crown on the Protestant <b>House of Hanover</b>, fears that Scotland might choose a different successor to the childless Queen Anne, together with Scotland’s heavy losses in the failed Darien scheme, led both parliaments to negotiate a full union.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Union became urgent after the English Act of Settlement of 1701 fixed the succession on the Protestant <b>House of Hanover</b>, while the Scottish Parliament’s Act of Security of 1704 reserved the right to choose a different successor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1698 the Civil List left the monarch dependent on Parliament for income, and the Act of Settlement 1701 fixed a Protestant succession, which brought the <b>House of Hanover</b> to the throne in 1714.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
